From 01c98c60e4df28ef9c25d7861965fbc4e39dbf53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 18:03:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#99): recover the suppressed tile when a weave
 submit misses
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A frame with inline-3D rects suppresses every canvas resource from the page
raster on the compositor thread (display.cc), and it does so before the weave
outcome can be known — the weave happens later, on the GPU thread. Patch 0057
then, correctly, refuses the phase (B) draw-back on a frame that wove nothing.
So that frame draws the tile from neither side: the quad was withheld AND the
woven texture was not blitted, and the tile shows the root pass's backdrop.
That is browser#99's black flicker. Patch 0047 predicted it in its own commit
message — "if a frame ever has inline-3D rects but the weave does not land,
this blanks rather than glitches" — and 0057 did not cause it: the stale
texture it rightly stopped blitting had been accidentally masking it.

Fill the hole on the GPU thread, where the miss is discovered. Immediately
before the liveness gate's return, draw each suppressed canvas resource
directly into the output surface at its tile rect — MONO, the left eye of the
side-by-side stretched across the whole tile. Everything needed is already in
hand: |inline_3d_canvas_layers_| is exactly the set display.cc suppressed, each
entry carrying the resolved mailbox, the unclipped tile rect and the quad's UV
window. Mono is the same flat fallback the SDK itself shows when 3D is
unavailable, and it is the only honest choice of the three: black is the bug,
and a naive full-UV draw would show the raw SBS squeezed to half width.

The details that matter:

- The left half is taken from the quad's EXISTING uv window, not from the whole
  texture (browser#73). For an atlased or partially-mapped resource those are
  different rectangles, and halving the texture samples another tile's pixels.
- The clip stays on the DESTINATION, the same browser#73 rule the weave input
  follows: a viewport-clipped WebGL canvas resource's physical texture can be
  shorter than the SkImage claims, so a source crop edge-clamps into a downward
  smear. Draw the unclipped tile rect and let Skia clip it.
- LINEAR sampling, unlike phase (B)'s device-pixel-exact NEAREST: this is a 2x
  horizontal magnification of an eye half, not a 1:1 interlace-lattice blit.
- The early-return path did NO flush of its own — phase (B)'s bracket sits
  below the return and is never reached — so the recovery frame brings its own
  FlushSurface + submit, and holds every representation and read access in
  vectors until after it. That is the 0064 lesson (an access released before
  the submit draws nothing), scaled from two objects to N tiles.
- Tiles clipped to empty by the window are skipped: no hole on screen, nothing
  lost. Tiles with nothing in hand stay black and are counted — note that
  display.cc's resolve() already DROPS a layer whose ResourceId has no mailbox
  while still suppressing it, so that class cannot reach this loop at all.

The block is unreachable when the frame wove, so it costs nothing in the steady
state. Batch mode only, like the gate it precedes: async mode keeps the last
woven output visible by contract and has no hole to fill. The gate is also the
single exit for the SubmitBatchSync RPC failure (batch_ok folds into
wove_this_frame just above it), so one site covers both miss classes. No
runtime change, no mojom change, no aggregator change.

One throttled VLOG(1) reports n= repaired / missing= not — browser#99's triage
plan asked for exactly that number, the direct measurement of how often we
punch a hole.

Also make the batch-submit-failure LOG(ERROR) throttled rather than capped at
three occurrences EVER, and append the running total. That line is the direct
signal for this failure mode, and it went permanently quiet after the first few
— making exactly the long flickering sessions #99 reports undiagnosable. First
three, then 1-in-300 (~5 s at 60 fps); the text up to the count is unchanged so
existing greps keep matching.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc      |  17 ++-
 .../skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc        | 138 ++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 152 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc b/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc
index fc1caa6fd1db1..a5665b2f0e329 100644
--- a/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc
+++ b/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc
@@ -693,11 +693,22 @@ bool DisplayXRWeaveGpu::SubmitBatchSync() {
       /*input_is_dxgi=*/true, overlay_handle, /*overlay_is_dxgi=*/true, flat);
   const base::TimeDelta dt = base::TimeTicks::Now() - t0;
   if (!sr.ok) {
+    // (browser#99) THROTTLED, not capped-at-3-ever. This line is the direct
+    // signal for the black-flicker mechanism — a failed submit is what trips
+    // the liveness gate and (pre-#99) punched the suppression hole — so a
+    // session that starts flickering ten minutes in must still leave a trace.
+    // The old `s_fail < 3` went permanently quiet after the first few and made
+    // exactly the long sessions that matter undiagnosable (#99's triage note).
+    // First 3 (a startup failure is never throttled away), then 1-in-300
+    // (~5 s at 60 fps). Text shape is deliberately unchanged up to the count,
+    // so existing greps for "[DisplayXR] batch weave submit failed (n=" keep
+    // matching; the running total is appended because #99 asked for the cap to
+    // become a counter.
     static int s_fail = 0;
-    if (s_fail < 3) {
-      ++s_fail;
+    const int n = s_fail++;
+    if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
       LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] batch weave submit failed (n=" << rects.size()
-                 << ")";
+                 << " fails=" << (n + 1) << ")";
     }
     return false;
   }
diff --git a/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc b/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc
index 01926790756ec..43b8034bb98fa 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc
@@ -3589,7 +3589,145 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
   // means "nothing woven". Async mode deliberately keeps the last woven output
   // visible while a submit is in flight — that is its one-frame-lag contract.
   if (batch && !wove_this_frame) {
+    // ---- (B') RECOVERY DRAW (browser#99): degrade to mono, never to black ----
+    //
+    // The gate above is correct — a stale woven texture must not be blitted —
+    // but returning bare leaves a HOLE, and that hole is browser#99's black
+    // flicker. The two halves of the mechanism are decided in different places
+    // and neither can see the other:
+    //   1. Display::DrawAndSwap suppressed every canvas resource from the page
+    //      raster (display.cc, keyed only on "this frame has rects"), on the
+    //      COMPOSITOR thread, before the weave outcome is knowable.
+    //   2. this early return then skips phase (B), the draw-back that was
+    //      supposed to put those pixels back.
+    // Net: the tile is drawn by nobody and shows the root pass's backdrop —
+    // black. Patch 0047 predicted this exact asymmetry in its own commit
+    // message ("if a frame ever has inline-3D rects but the weave does not
+    // land, this blanks rather than glitches"); 0057 made it visible by
+    // (rightly) refusing the stale blit that used to mask it.
+    //
+    // So paint the tiles ourselves this frame. Everything needed is already in
+    // hand: |inline_3d_canvas_layers_| is exactly the set display.cc
+    // suppressed, each entry carrying the resolved mailbox, the unclipped tile
+    // rect and the quad's UV window. We draw MONO — the left eye of the
+    // side-by-side, stretched across the whole tile. That is the same flat
+    // fallback the SDK itself shows when 3D is unavailable, and it is the only
+    // honest choice of the three: black is the bug, and a naive full-UV draw
+    // would show the raw SBS horizontally squeezed to half width.
+    //
+    // This block is unreachable on a healthy frame — |wove_this_frame| true
+    // skips it outright — so it costs nothing in the steady state. Batch mode
+    // only, like the gate: async mode keeps the last woven output visible by
+    // contract, so it has no hole to fill.
+    //
+    // The representations and read accesses must outlive the flush+submit
+    // below (the 0064 lesson — Ganesh reads the texture at flush time, so an
+    // access released before the submit draws nothing), hence the vectors:
+    // they are the same idiom as |woven_access| / |over_access| in phase (B),
+    // scaled to N tiles.
+    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<gpu::SkiaImageRepresentation>> rec_reps;
+    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<gpu::SkiaImageRepresentation::ScopedReadAccess>>
+        rec_accesses;
+    rec_reps.reserve(inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size());
+    rec_accesses.reserve(inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size());
+    int rec_drawn = 0;
+    int rec_missing = 0;
+    for (const auto& c : inline_3d_canvas_layers_) {
+      // Clipped to nothing by the window: there is no hole on screen to fill.
+      // Not counted as missing — nothing was lost.
+      gfx::Rect on_screen = c.rect;
+      on_screen.Intersect(window_bounds);
+      if (on_screen.IsEmpty()) {
+        continue;
+      }
+      // Nothing in hand -> this tile stays black; count it so the log below
+      // separates "we repaired it" from "we could not". Note display.cc's
+      // resolve() already DROPS layers whose ResourceId has no mailbox while
+      // still suppressing them, so that class never reaches this loop and
+      // cannot be counted here; this guard covers the rest.
+      if (c.mailbox.IsZero()) {
+        ++rec_missing;
+        continue;
+      }
+      auto rep = shared_image_representation_factory_->ProduceSkia(
+          c.mailbox, context_state_.get());
+      if (!rep) {
+        ++rec_missing;
+        continue;
+      }
+      std::vector<GrBackendSemaphore> rbegin_sem, rend_sem;
+      auto access = rep->BeginScopedReadAccess(&rbegin_sem, &rend_sem);
+      if (!access) {
+        ++rec_missing;
+        continue;
+      }
+      sk_sp<SkImage> img = access->CreateSkImage(context_state_.get());
+      if (!img) {
+        ++rec_missing;
+        continue;
+      }
+      if (!rbegin_sem.empty()) {
+        output->wait(rbegin_sem.size(), rbegin_sem.data(),
+                     /*deleteSemaphoresAfterWait=*/false);
+      }
+      // LEFT EYE = the left half of the quad's EXISTING UV WINDOW, not of the
+      // whole texture (browser#73): |uv| is the normalized sub-rect of the
+      // resource that |rect| displays, and for an atlased or partially-mapped
+      // canvas resource the two are not the same rectangle. Halving the
+      // texture instead would sample the wrong pixels — often another tile's.
+      gfx::RectF uvw = c.uv;
+      if (uvw.IsEmpty()) {
+        uvw = gfx::RectF(0.f, 0.f, 1.f, 1.f);
+      }
+      const float iw = img->width();
+      const float ih = img->height();
+      const SkRect src = SkRect::MakeXYWH(uvw.x() * iw, uvw.y() * ih,
+                                          uvw.width() * 0.5f * iw,
+                                          uvw.height() * ih);
+      // Destination is the UNCLIPPED tile rect, exactly where the page would
+      // have drawn the quad; Skia clips it to the surface. This is the same
+      // browser#73 rule the weave input follows — express a clip on the
+      // DESTINATION, never by sub-regioning the source, because a
+      // viewport-clipped WebGL canvas resource's physical texture can be
+      // shorter than the SkImage claims and a source crop edge-clamps into a
+      // downward smear.
+      const SkRect dst = gfx::RectToSkRect(c.rect);
+      SkPaint paint;
+      paint.setBlendMode(SkBlendMode::kSrcOver);
+      // LINEAR, unlike phase (B): this is a 2x horizontal magnification of the
+      // eye half, not the device-pixel-exact 1:1 blit of an interlace lattice.
+      // NEAREST here would show the un-squeeze as visible column doubling.
+      out_canvas->drawImageRect(img.get(), src, dst,
+                                SkSamplingOptions(SkFilterMode::kLinear),
+                                &paint, SkCanvas::kStrict_SrcRectConstraint);
+      ++rec_drawn;
+      rec_reps.push_back(std::move(rep));
+      rec_accesses.push_back(std::move(access));
+    }
+    // The early-return path did NO flush of its own — phase (B)'s bracket is
+    // below this return and we never reach it — so the recovery frame has to
+    // bring its own, mirroring how phase (B) ends.
+    if (rec_drawn > 0) {
+      std::vector<GrBackendSemaphore> empty_sem;
+      FlushSurface(output, empty_sem, /*scoped_write_access=*/nullptr);
+      if (gr_context()) {
+        gr_context()->submit();
+      }
+      if (graphite_shared_context()) {
+        graphite_shared_context()->submit();
+      }
+    }
+    // browser#99's triage plan asked for exactly this number: how often are we
+    // punching a hole, and how many of those holes could we not repair.
+    // Throttled 1-in-60 (~once a second at 60 fps) — a frame that reaches here
+    // is by definition an unhealthy one, so it must not be able to flood.
+    static int rec_dbg = 0;
+    if ((rec_dbg++ % 60) == 0) {
+      VLOG(1) << "[DisplayXR] inline-3D recovery draw: n=" << rec_drawn
+              << " missing=" << rec_missing;
+    }
     return;
+    // rec_accesses / rec_reps release here, after the submit.
   }
 
   // ---- (B) Draw back the accumulated window-sized woven output (Windows) ----
-- 
2.42.0.windows.2

